<Base title="About" description="Why Open Repair Record exists, who started it, and how it is licensed." section="about">
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Why this exists</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        "Completed" means something different to every contractor, every company and every system a repair passes
        through. Open Repair Record gives it one shape that all of them can read.
      </p>

      <h2>One word, many meanings</h2>
      <p>Ask four people involved in the same repair when it is complete, and you get four answers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The contractor:</strong> when they leave. The work they were sent for is done, as far as they can tell.</li>
        <li><strong>The coordinator:</strong> when the ticket can be closed and the invoice approved.</li>
        <li><strong>The resident:</strong> when the problem stops, and stays stopped.</li>
        <li><strong>The owner:</strong> when they know what was done, what it cost, and that it will not be back next month.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Contractors differ among themselves too. One technician writes "done" after a full test. Another writes "done"
        after a temporary fix, meaning "done for today". A third closes the job when the part is ordered. The word is
        the same; the work behind it is not.
      </p>

      <h2>Every system has its own "done"</h2>
      <p>
        Systems add their own meanings. A contractor's app may mark a job <em>Completed</em> when the technician taps a
        button on site. A helpdesk may mark it <em>Resolved</em> when the contractor's e-mail arrives. A CAFM may close
        it automatically after a few days without news. A spreadsheet just says <em>done</em>. When one repair passes
        through the property manager's system, the FM company's CAFM and a subcontractor's app, the same status is set
        three times, and nobody can say which meaning it carries.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result is familiar. The job shows as completed everywhere. A week later the resident writes that the leak is
        back. And nobody can tell from the records what was claimed, what was checked, and what the resident ever said.
      </p>

      <h2>One shape, whatever the system</h2>
      <p>
        Open Repair Record does not replace any of these tools, and it does not try to settle whose "done" is right.
        It writes down the parts separately: who says the work is done and what exactly they did, what was checked and
        what was not, what the resident answered, and who closed the record and why. Each company keeps its own system;
        the record is what they can hand to each other, as a page anyone can read or as a
        <a href="/format/">small JSON file</a> any system can import.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <p>
        Open Repair Record was initiated by the team behind <a href="https://pmfriend.com/">PMFriend</a>, the AI bridge
        between property management and facility management. PMFriend automates the whole repair loop, from the first
        message to the resident's confirmation, and the work around it, from compliance certificates to owner reports.
        The guide and this tool are deliberately useful without it: no account, no PMFriend footer on your reports,
        and anyone, including other software vendors, is welcome to use and implement the format.
      </p>

